.TH MAIL 5
.SH NAME
\&.mail \- the mail accounts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ~/.mail
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR mail (1)
and the desktop's Mail window read the user's accounts from
.BR ~/.mail :
.IB key = value
lines, only the user's to read (it holds passwords).
A line starting with
.B [
(such as
.BR [account] )
starts the next account; the first is account 0
.RB ( "mail \-a" " N"
picks another).
At most 8 accounts.
.TP
.B name
the name shown as the sender
.TP
.B address
the e-mail address
.TP
.B in
.B imap
or
.B pop3
.TP
.BR in_host ", " in_port
the server to read from; the port's default is 993 (IMAP) or 995 (POP3)
.TP
.BR out_host ", " out_port
the SMTP server to send through; default port 465
.TP
.BR user ", " pass
the login on both servers
.TP
.B keep
POP3: 1 (the default) leaves the messages on the server, 0 deletes them
.TP
.B sent
0: no copy in Sent (IMAP); a copy by default
.TP
.B selfsigned
1: a certificate no authority signed may be accepted: its SHA-256 is then
pinned in
.B in_pin
and
.B out_pin
(64 hex digits each), and any other refused
.TP
.B fav
the Mail window's favourite folders
.PP
Ports 993, 995 and 465 speak TLS at once; others (143, 110, 587) start in
clear and must switch to TLS (STARTTLS, STLS) before the password goes.
POP3 copies new messages into
.BR ~/Mail/INBOX ,
one file each, named by the server's UIDL.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
[account]
name=Alice Example
address=alice@example.org
in=imap
in_host=imap.example.org
out_host=smtp.example.org
user=alice@example.org
pass=secret
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mail (1),
.BR atlas (4)
